      "text": "diamagnetic, diamagnetic and paramagnetic",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In the structure&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>&nbsp;each&nbsp;lithium atom loses one electron to form 2 cations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Li</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;oxygen atom gains two electrons to form the anion&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math>.&nbsp; It has no unpaired electrons. So, it is diamagnetic in nature.&nbsp;</p><p>The peroxide&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>&nbsp;present in&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;has 4 electron in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>, thus all are paired. So, it is diamagnetic in nature.</p><p>In the structure&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;oxygen is present in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math>. The number of electrons present is 17, where the&nbsp;where the sixteen electrons are from two oxygen atoms and one from the negative charge. There is one unpaired electron present in the last orbital. Hence, it is paramagnetic in nature.&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;diamagnetic</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;diamagnetic</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;paramagnetic</p>",
